hostname core
!
ip routing
!
! ============================================================
! core — internal router, OSPF area 0
!
! Topology: [core] --OSPF area 0-- [asbr] --NOT in OSPF-- [internet]
!
! core represents an internal campus or enterprise router.
! It has no direct path to the internet and learns a default route
! via OSPF from the asbr. This is the normal "learned default" pattern.
! ============================================================

interface Loopback0
 ip address 10.0.0.1/32

! Link to asbr — segment 10.1.12.0/30 — OSPF area 0
! core is .1, asbr is .2
interface Ethernet1
 no switchport
 description Link to asbr (10.1.12.0/30, core=.1 asbr=.2) — OSPF area 0
 ip address 10.1.12.1/30

! ============================================================
! TODO: Configure OSPF
!
! Step 1 — Basic OSPF:
!   router ospf 1
!    ospf router-id 10.0.0.1
!    network 10.0.0.1/32 area 0
!    network 10.1.12.0/30 area 0
!    passive-interface Loopback0
!
! After asbr injects the default route, verify here:
!   show ip route
!     Look for: S* 0.0.0.0/0 (static) — NOT expected here, core has no static
!               O*E2 0.0.0.0/0 (OSPF external) — THIS is what you want to see
!
!   show ip ospf database external
!     Shows the Type-5 LSA generated by asbr for the default route
!
! Step 2 — Experiment: asbr removes its static default
!   When asbr has NO default route, `default-information originate` (without
!   `always`) will STOP advertising the default into OSPF.
!   Watch the O*E2 route disappear from core's routing table.
!
! Step 3 — Experiment: asbr uses `default-information originate always`
!   Even with no static default on asbr, `always` forces the advertisement.
!   The O*E2 0.0.0.0/0 reappears on core, but following it leads nowhere
!   (black hole). This is useful for testing and for scenarios where asbr
!   always knows a path to the internet by other means.
!
! Step 4 — Conditional default with route-map (on asbr):
!   asbr only injects the default if a specific prefix (e.g., 203.0.113.0/30)
!   is present in its routing table. This makes default origination conditional
!   on internet reachability rather than just the presence of a static route.
! ============================================================
